<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.26100.0</TargetFramework>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>10.0.19041.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <RootNamespace>Juice.Cli</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>juice</AssemblyName>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;win-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <!--
      Region and currency resolution depend on real globalization data: invariant mode
      makes RegionInfo.CurrentRegion useless, which breaks electricity rate lookup.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>
    <!--
      ReadyToRun is off here, but this setting has almost no effect on the shipped package,
      and that is worth explaining because the obvious reading is wrong.

      An MSIX layout is a single flat folder. The tray application's build supplies every
      shared assembly in it, the .NET runtime and the Windows API projection included, and
      pack.ps1 copies only four files out of this project's publish on top: juice.exe,
      juice.dll and the two json files. So whether this project precompiles decides the
      size and content of juice.dll alone, 0.03 MB of it, and nothing else.

      ReadyToRun is therefore a package-wide decision made by Juice.App, not a per-project
      one. It is enabled there. The packaged juice.exe loads that application's precompiled
      assemblies out of the shared folder and gets the benefit either way.

      Off is still the right setting, for two reasons that apply outside the package.
      Publishing this project standalone with it on costs 43 MB, the Windows API projection
      growing from 25 MB to 63 MB, which pack.ps1 pays for on every architecture and then
      discards. And it buys nothing measurable: median wall time for juice now is 2040 ms
      with it and 2050 ms without, over twelve interleaved runs of each with warm-up runs
      discarded, and the ranges overlap completely. Even the lightest command, sources,
      moves only from 933 ms to 907 ms. This tool is not startup bound. Every command
      samples hardware energy counters over a real time window, and that window dwarfs the
      jit work precompiling avoids.
    -->
    <PublishReadyToRun>false</PublishReadyToRun>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Juice.Core\Juice.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Juice.Platform.Windows\Juice.Platform.Windows.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
